            <ArticleTitle>Just when we thought we knew everything we needed to know about Zn acquisition and bacterial pathogenesis.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">JB.00694-17</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1128/JB.00694-17</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>It is well established that high affinity zinc importers play essential roles in bacterial virulence. But the studies described by Moreau et al. (1) in this issue of the Journal of Bacteriology demonstrate that we probably still have much to learn about how these transporters function and the genes that encode them are regulated in different bacterial pathogens.</AbstractText>
